VIOLATION — tenant-isolation.md MUST NOT, third bullet: building tenant-scoped infrastructure
without a tenant-aware invalidation entry point. `EmbeddingStore` partitions every namespace by
tenant on the write and read sides and exposes exactly one clear-side method, `flush()`, which drops
the whole index. The only way to clear one customer's embeddings is to clear everyone's, which is
the state the bullet forbids, and the rationale given is the bullet's own consequence restated as
acceptable: re-embedding is lazy, so a global flush is cheap. The transcript also supplies its own
counter-evidence — three re-ingest tickets last quarter — and still defers the scoped purge to a
follow-up, so the store ships with no tenant-aware clear path.

DISCRIMINATORS. This is not MUST-2 and not the first MUST NOT bullet: `_namespace` raises
`TenantRequiredError` when nothing is bound and substitutes no placeholder anywhere, so the
unbound-tenant contract is held. It is not MUST-3 either — MUST-3 governs an existing invalidation
entry point accepting a tenant parameter, whereas the defect here is that no tenant-aware entry
point exists at all on newly-built infrastructure. GlobalGlossary's tenant-free namespace is correct:
that collection is declared single-tenant. MUST-1 is not in play — the multi-tenant namespaces all
carry the tenant segment.
